WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.620
Mature NGF keeps neurons alive.

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.460
So how could it kill cells that lack TrkA?

3
00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.310
And why did the precursor kill neurons in one lab, but keep them weakly alive in

4
00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:15.310
another?

5
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:18.830
Simple binding arithmetic helps with the first question.

6
00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.330
The precursor needs its pro-domain.

7
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:27.303
Binding studies on chick sensory neurons found two classes of NGF site.

8
00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:32.847
Their dissociation constants were about 20 pM and 2 nM.

9
00:00:33.817 --> 00:00:38.617
Treat these as orders of magnitude, about ten picomolar and one nanomolar.

10
00:00:39.437 --> 00:00:41.757
The two kinds of site are a hundredfold apart.

11
00:00:42.717 --> 00:00:47.957
A dissociation constant, K d, is the free concentration that fills half the sites.

12
00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:50.177
Below it, fewer than half fill.

13
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:55.390
Pick a low level for illustration, ten picomolar of mature NGF.

14
00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:57.290
Which sites fill?

15
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.370
About half of the Trk sites fill.

16
00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.130
Only about one percent of the p75 sites do.

17
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:15.200
So at low levels, a much larger fraction of the high-affinity sites fills than of

18
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.260
the p75 sites. Survival depends on signals this count leaves out.

19
00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:23.403
Now turn the dial to about 2 nM.

20
00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:27.343
Culture experiments often add NGF in this range.

21
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:30.033
The Trk sites are nearly full.

22
00:01:30.613 --> 00:01:34.453
And roughly half to two thirds of the p75 sites fill too.

23
00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:39.433
So in a dish, mature NGF fills many p75 sites.

24
00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.653
That fits it killing TrkA-lacking cells there.

25
00:01:43.617 --> 00:01:46.117
But filling a site is not the same as signaling.

26
00:01:47.017 --> 00:01:49.677
The model has no threshold for survival or death.

27
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:52.507
Nor can it explain the precursor.

28
00:01:52.507 --> 00:01:57.447
At a low dose where mature NGF added no death, the precursor killed.

29
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:01.407
What the precursor adds is the pro-domain.

30
00:02:01.407 --> 00:02:06.347
Sortilin grips it, and in the cells tested, the precursor's death signal needed

31
00:02:06.347 --> 00:02:07.347
sortilin.

32
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.200
This lesson puts no binding numbers on proNGF.

33
00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.220
Its effects are compared by experiment instead.

34
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.810
So the cut shifts the balance.

35
00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.830
It does not flip a switch.

36
00:02:19.883 --> 00:02:24.303
Why did the precursor kill in one lab, and support weak survival in another?

37
00:02:25.263 --> 00:02:26.263
That is still open.

38
00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:32.073
The dose, the receptor balance, and how much precursor was cut during the experiment

39
00:02:32.073 --> 00:02:33.073
could each matter.

40
00:02:33.883 --> 00:02:36.143
Three variables now go a long way.

41
00:02:37.103 --> 00:02:38.623
First, whether the factor has been cut.

42
00:02:39.583 --> 00:02:41.823
Second, which receptors the cell makes.

43
00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:43.783
Third, how much of each.

44
00:02:44.683 --> 00:02:47.803
Change any one, and the same factor can mean something else.

45
00:02:48.817 --> 00:02:49.877
Remember the rule.

46
00:02:50.797 --> 00:02:52.177
The target supplies the factor.

47
00:02:53.117 --> 00:02:54.657
The receptors decide what it means.

48
00:02:55.650 --> 00:03:00.190
Next, see how competing neurons use p75 against each other.

49
00:03:01.183 --> 00:03:03.563
The takeaway: dose changes which sites fill.

50
00:03:04.343 --> 00:03:07.563
The cut changes which receptors the factor works through best.
